+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+You can show progress while reading a file with :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open`, or with the module-level ``open`` function, which creates its own display. The module-level function returns a context manager. The following example reads a file as text and shows progress while it is read::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.json") as file:
+        contents = file.read()
+
+The default mode is ``'r'``, which returns text. Use ``'rb'`` to read bytes instead::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.bin", "rb") as file:
+        contents = file.read()
+
+The total is the size of the file in bytes, which is found automatically. Progress is counted in bytes, whichever mode you use.
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have a binary file object, use :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` on a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, or the module-level ``wrap_file`` function. Only binary files are supported. You must give the total size in bytes, unless you pass an existing task with a total to :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`. The reader does not close the file you wrap, so you still need to close it yourself::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as handle:
+        with rich.progress.wrap_file(handle, 1024) as file:
+            contents = file.read()
+
